# Three.js / 2D game rules

- Prefer a clear scene hierarchy and naming (e.g. groups for logical parts) so objects can be found and disposed correctly.
- Dispose of geometries, materials, and textures when no longer needed to avoid memory leaks.
- For postprocessing: keep pass order explicit and document (e.g. comment or constant) so changes don't break expected visual order.
- In 2D game code: keep game logic (state, rules, scoring) separate from render/input where possible for testability and clarity.
- Avoid unnecessary draw calls and heavy per-frame allocations; reuse objects and buffers where it helps performance.
- **R3F/Drei patterns:** When using React Three Fiber, use `useLoader` (cached) instead of manual loaders; use `<Suspense>` for async assets; keep scene graph components pure and side-effect-free.
- **Rapier physics:** Set fixed timestep via `<Physics timeStep="vary">` for determinism; pair `RigidBody` with a matching collider; clean up with `useEffect` return in R3F.
- **WebGPU/TSL (Three.js r167+):** Use `WebGPURenderer` with TSL node materials when targeting WebGPU; provide a WebGL fallback via `WebGLRenderer` for unsupported browsers.
